WEBVTT

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:06.520
What we found was that first of all, pilots look for their

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:10.560
reserve handle along their skeleton.

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:14.520
So if they're rotating forwards, their hand naturally goes to

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:17.480
their hip bone. If they're rotating backwards as

00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:21.000
they would in the SAT on auto rotation, they go a bit further

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:25.400
down their thigh. We also found that one of the

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:29.720
best pieces of advice I was given by a senior doctor was

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:32.200
don't just do something man, stand there.

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:36.160
So the opposite of the don't just stand there, do something.

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:43.360
It's the idea of take some time, soak in what's happened, think

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:48.400
about your options and make the right decision psychologically.

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:51.440
It's also been shown that if you're able to integrate the

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:55.840
experience and understand why something happened, then you're

00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:58.240
less likely to find it traumatising.

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:03.320
Doc, I have heard your name time and again at various places and

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:06.440
you know, one thing that has come to my mind a lot of times

00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:12.560
is that Physiology of the art of light is kind of very different

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:14.760
from the Physiology of usual sports.

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:20.960
So what I'm wondering is that how important is the the

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:24.360
physical aspect in paragliding, even though it is not a very

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:26.680
physically demanding sport as we look at it?

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:31.000
I think it's a really interesting question because a

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:34.360
lot of us come from a sports background and so when we think

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:37.360
about training for anything, we think, right, I've got to go for

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:39.000
a run, I've got to lift some weights, I've got to do all this

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:43.920
stuff. And when we're in the air, we're

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:46.600
still subjected to demanding conditions.

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:49.920
You know, often it's cold or it might be a high altitude or

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:53.800
there might be G forces. We might have to run hard for

00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:57.640
take off, but the kind of loading on the body is

00:01:57.640 --> 00:01:59.280
different. You know, it's kind of light on

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:01.400
the legs but very heavy on the brain.

00:02:01.960 --> 00:02:06.880
And so the way I think about it is that Physiology is important

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:10.720
because it sets us up to make the right decisions and do the

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:14.600
right things. And optimizing Physiology kind

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:17.960
of makes sense because if you want to fly well, you want to

00:02:17.960 --> 00:02:20.280
get your brain in the best possible state.

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:23.600
But it's not kind of like it would be if I'm going to run a

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:27.000
marathon where I need to just be actively training my my heart

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:28.440
and my legs. OK.

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:30.640
I just want to look at it from a business perspective because

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:32.960
this is something that I experienced myself very

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:36.600
intimately. And the thing was when I started

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:39.760
seeing the world from above, which is a completely different

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:42.360
perspective, which you're not used to because obviously

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:45.680
commercial flights does give you that maybe a little bit while

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:48.120
take off and landing, but you're just a spectator.

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:50.000
You're not controlling the aircraft.

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:52.760
There's no decision making. But the moment you start doing

00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:56.440
that in your first few high altitude flights, it's a lot of

00:02:56.440 --> 00:02:58.440
processing that your brain has to do.

00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:04.640
So the the cognitive function of the brain is, is there a certain

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:10.600
period of a paraglider's flight or, or like the learning where

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:15.560
the cognitive brain is adapting to this brand new sensory input

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:19.240
that the eyes are giving it and that's why it's using more than

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:22.240
the usual energy that it would once you kind of settle down and

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:25.280
this becomes like once you don't get goosebumps every time you

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:28.840
get in the air basically. I think that's such a good

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:32.000
question and there's so much you can pick apart.

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:37.440
So I guess I think about this quite broadly initially.

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:39.680
It's like, what's the context in which we're flying?

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:44.440
Because I'm always thinking about how can we do it better or

00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:47.520
safer or, or more enjoyably, You know, it's all our hobbies.

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:50.360
So most importantly is that we're, we're having a good time.

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:55.600
And so when we think about going flying, you know, if we're going

00:03:55.600 --> 00:03:59.840
flying for one day, then that's going to be a different set of

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:02.880
demands to if we're going flying day after day after day in a

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:05.960
comp. And you kind of feel that in the

00:04:05.960 --> 00:04:09.360
cognitive loading, don't you? Like you feel the fatigue over

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:13.680
the course of days. Sometimes somebody's think, oh,

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:16.200
I actually need a day off to, to clear my mind.

00:04:17.720 --> 00:04:21.519
The other thing, as you point out, is the kind of the idea of

00:04:21.519 --> 00:04:23.720
bandwidth. You know that when you, as you

00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:26.400
say, when you first fly, you get goosebumps and you can't really

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:31.280
think about anything else. But as we start to automate our

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:35.000
actions, as we get, you know, the flying part, you know, when

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:36.960
you're a beginner, you're just consumed by which string do I

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:39.440
pull and how do I land where I need to land.

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:43.720
When that becomes less consuming, then you start to

00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:47.200
think more about the world around you, about thermals,

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:51.120
thinking forwards in terms of what needs to happen next, and

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:53.400
also thinking back in terms of how you can draw on your

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:56.480
experience. So to my mind, it's almost like

00:04:56.480 --> 00:05:00.400
the, you know, I can't necessarily say that we like we

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:05.280
grow a paragliding cortex, but what I think we do is we shift

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:10.080
the act of paragliding to different parts of the brain as

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:14.080
we progress. So the sort of actions, the

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:17.480
physical actions, you know, get shifted to more of a

00:05:17.480 --> 00:05:20.360
subconscious part of the brain. You know, the, the kind of

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:25.200
movement patterns will get embedded in, I suppose, less

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:29.760
kind of cognitively demanding parts, opening that up to some

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:33.160
of the, the bigger questions. The other kind of interesting

00:05:33.160 --> 00:05:35.560
thing that you said there though, is, is that the brain

00:05:35.560 --> 00:05:39.720
needs more energy. And this is kind of a, it's kind

00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:44.760
of an interesting fallacy, which is the brain uses 20% of our

00:05:44.760 --> 00:05:46.120
energy. That's kind of a fact that we

00:05:46.120 --> 00:05:49.280
know. But thinking hard doesn't

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:53.760
actually use that much more like the energy that the brain is

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:56.280
using is is really just trying to kind of maintain it in this

00:05:56.280 --> 00:06:00.120
constant state where all the nerves are just busy shifting

00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:02.960
around potassium, sodium and doing all the things that they

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:06.720
do. But we all know that if you've

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:09.880
got to think hard to do something cognitively demanding,

00:06:10.240 --> 00:06:13.440
then if you don't eat that day, you're going to do worse.

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:16.400
And we also know that if you work really hard at something

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:18.480
all day, you get fatigued and tired.

00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:24.320
And So what that's really about is avoiding big spikes in blood

00:06:24.320 --> 00:06:28.480
glucose. So if you're eating regularly,

00:06:28.480 --> 00:06:30.120
you're keeping yourself hydrated.

00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:33.160
It's not that the brain needs extra energy and you're giving

00:06:33.160 --> 00:06:35.600
it that extra energy. It's you're keeping a really

00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:39.120
nice constant energy environment for it to draw on.

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:44.400
And I think that's important because when people are flying,

00:06:45.160 --> 00:06:49.200
sometimes it's tempting to like not eat for a while or to wait

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:52.200
until you're tired or wait until you you feel like you need to

00:06:52.200 --> 00:06:54.520
eat. But actually by just eating

00:06:54.520 --> 00:06:57.720
regularly and often, even if you're not thinking that hard at

00:06:57.720 --> 00:07:00.120
the beginning of the flight, for example, you're just kind of

00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:02.920
chilling and cruising waiting for the comp to start, what

00:07:02.920 --> 00:07:05.920
you're doing is maintaining that really nice constant energy

00:07:05.920 --> 00:07:08.920
environment. So there's always a nice even

00:07:08.920 --> 00:07:12.200
supply of glucose in your blood. There aren't big spikes or big

00:07:12.200 --> 00:07:15.880
crashes, so the brain can just draw from it as it needs.

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:20.920
Is there a certain food or type of food which is the most

00:07:20.920 --> 00:07:28.080
optimal brain fuel which you can intake during a strenuous like

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:35.720
cognitively heavy activity? So I think it's really about two

00:07:35.720 --> 00:07:37.040
things. First is just the

00:07:37.040 --> 00:07:40.320
practicalities, like something that is practical to eat in the

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:43.680
air. But the second is, you know,

00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:47.480
kind of to your point at the beginning, like this isn't

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:51.560
normal sport where you're burning tons and tons of

00:07:51.560 --> 00:07:55.840
glucose. So smashing like an energy gel

00:07:55.840 --> 00:07:59.920
or, you know, loads of sugar or other things like that, that's

00:07:59.920 --> 00:08:02.240
probably more likely to create spikes.

00:08:02.600 --> 00:08:06.320
So my take on it is actually you want kind of complex

00:08:06.320 --> 00:08:10.160
carbohydrates, so things that are broken down relatively

00:08:10.160 --> 00:08:14.120
slowly. So, you know, kind of bread or

00:08:14.120 --> 00:08:19.840
cereal bars or things like that. That's kind of that's probably

00:08:19.840 --> 00:08:23.520
the scientific answer, but I know for a fact that sometime if

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:28.320
I'm about to do something a bit stressful, then a few Haribo

00:08:28.600 --> 00:08:32.080
seems to make me feel better. And I can't really explain that

00:08:32.080 --> 00:08:36.120
by biochemical means, but I know by experience that that is what

00:08:36.120 --> 00:08:37.960
I crave at that point was an M&M's.

00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:40.159
So I think the scientific answer to your question would be

00:08:40.159 --> 00:08:43.159
complex carbohydrates that are easy to eat in the air.

00:08:43.679 --> 00:08:47.280
The practical answer is kind of what makes you feel good at that

00:08:47.280 --> 00:08:49.360
point in time. That's yeah, like because feel

00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:50.800
good factor is the bottom line, right?

00:08:50.800 --> 00:08:53.680
Like if it feels good, then it's like whether it's placebo or

00:08:53.680 --> 00:08:56.640
not, but it's going to make you believe that it's good and

00:08:56.640 --> 00:08:59.840
you're kind of more likely to sail through that thought and

00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:03.960
eventually make it happen. I think so.

00:09:03.960 --> 00:09:07.560
I guess one of the things that this draws on is that it's quite

00:09:07.920 --> 00:09:14.200
hard to give paragliding specific answers based on the

00:09:14.200 --> 00:09:16.760
science. And I try, and I've tried to do

00:09:16.760 --> 00:09:20.960
some of the science, but if you think about it, paragliding is

00:09:20.960 --> 00:09:25.720
an incredibly complex activity. It's complex cognitively, but

00:09:25.720 --> 00:09:27.640
it's really complex environmentally.

00:09:28.440 --> 00:09:33.320
And so kind of classically, when you study stuff, you measure

00:09:33.320 --> 00:09:37.280
something in the Physiology like a drop in oxygen saturation and

00:09:37.280 --> 00:09:42.320
then you do some cognitive tests like, you know, can you do a

00:09:42.320 --> 00:09:44.520
puzzle faster or more accurately?

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:48.680
And then you generalise what that will mean for behaviour.

00:09:49.640 --> 00:09:56.120
Now in a really simple task that is a chain that's quite easy to

00:09:56.120 --> 00:09:59.920
make. But in paragliding, you know,

00:10:01.000 --> 00:10:04.720
it's something that paragliding could be a top to bottom or it

00:10:04.720 --> 00:10:08.640
could be a competition over 100 kilometres, or it could be

00:10:08.640 --> 00:10:11.480
acrobatics. It could be somebody who's a

00:10:11.760 --> 00:10:14.040
beginner, somebody who's incredibly experienced.

00:10:14.040 --> 00:10:17.440
It could be somebody who is feeling calm and chill or

00:10:17.440 --> 00:10:19.920
somebody who's had no sleep the night before.

00:10:21.200 --> 00:10:23.840
It could be somebody at the start of the season, at the end

00:10:23.840 --> 00:10:26.680
of the season. You know, all of these things.

00:10:27.480 --> 00:10:31.440
The variation in within individuals and the variation

00:10:31.440 --> 00:10:35.680
amongst individuals means it's quite hard to give definitive

00:10:35.680 --> 00:10:37.840
answers and it's just quite hard to study.

00:10:38.520 --> 00:10:41.800
And so, and also because there aren't very many paraglider

00:10:41.800 --> 00:10:45.320
pilots, that means sample sizes are quite small.

00:10:45.560 --> 00:10:47.400
There's also not tons of money in it.

00:10:47.400 --> 00:10:49.720
You know, I've done all my research kind of on the side as

00:10:49.720 --> 00:10:52.560
a hobby really, because I love the sport and I enjoy doing the

00:10:52.560 --> 00:10:56.240
science. But it means that we don't have,

00:10:56.360 --> 00:11:01.120
say, the huge banks of data that we would have in running, for

00:11:01.120 --> 00:11:06.520
example, or football. So I guess all the answers that

00:11:06.520 --> 00:11:08.600
I'm going to try and give during this and all the conclusion I'm

00:11:08.600 --> 00:11:12.120
trying to give doing this are the best collection of thoughts

00:11:12.120 --> 00:11:14.480
and ideas I've had drawing on all this other literature,

00:11:14.480 --> 00:11:18.120
sports stuff, environmental Physiology stuff, bit of

00:11:18.120 --> 00:11:21.320
military things, you know, all of these things I'll try and

00:11:21.320 --> 00:11:24.800
bring together. But I was a great big pinch of

00:11:24.800 --> 00:11:27.720
salt on all of my answers because none of us really know.

00:11:27.720 --> 00:11:31.200
But we've just got to try. I think even the manufacturers

00:11:31.360 --> 00:11:34.560
which probably make the most money or let's say the software

00:11:34.560 --> 00:11:38.840
people like such instrument manufacturers, even they are not

00:11:38.840 --> 00:11:42.360
in it for making huge amounts of cash, but like the amount of

00:11:42.360 --> 00:11:45.520
satisfaction it goes. So regardless of how small it

00:11:45.520 --> 00:11:48.280
is, there is something that it gives back which is so valuable

00:11:48.280 --> 00:11:50.240
that kind of keeps all of us in as well.

00:11:50.520 --> 00:11:52.800
Oh I couldn't agree more. I've not been able to fly for a

00:11:52.800 --> 00:11:54.360
few weeks. I'm already getting frustrated

00:11:54.360 --> 00:11:56.000
and I'm already missing my flying friends.

00:11:56.080 --> 00:11:57.920
I couldn't agree more. But I'm more talk about the

00:11:57.920 --> 00:12:02.480
cashier in the sense that sadly research is a job like any other

00:12:02.520 --> 00:12:04.680
and so researched and to go where the money is.

00:12:04.680 --> 00:12:07.880
So when in these small niche sports, it's really hard to get

00:12:07.880 --> 00:12:09.920
stuff done that is directly relevant.

00:12:10.080 --> 00:12:12.240
I was thinking from the perspective of, let's say, a

00:12:12.240 --> 00:12:15.120
pilot who wants to go out and make it big.

00:12:15.200 --> 00:12:18.640
And when it comes to because I don't know if competing is

00:12:18.640 --> 00:12:20.760
inherent when you're younger, because a lot, a lot of

00:12:21.320 --> 00:12:24.640
conventional sports kids starting from pre teens to

00:12:24.640 --> 00:12:28.280
getting into competitive circles and then investing all in, of

00:12:28.280 --> 00:12:30.840
course, for the help of whatever support system they have.

00:12:31.120 --> 00:12:36.920
And then reaching a fair bit of high level of exertion, mental,

00:12:36.920 --> 00:12:40.240
physical, like all kind of training by the late teens.

00:12:40.240 --> 00:12:44.400
And that kind of sets up a very solid foundation for them to

00:12:44.400 --> 00:12:49.240
peak or be in a position to peak when they are in their mid 20s

00:12:49.240 --> 00:12:51.120
or something. And then some of them get on to

00:12:51.120 --> 00:12:52.520
be legends or those kind of things.

00:12:52.960 --> 00:12:56.760
I do remember Russ Ogden saying that paragliders peak around the

00:12:56.760 --> 00:13:00.400
age of 30 and probably I think one of the main reasons for that

00:13:00.400 --> 00:13:03.600
could be that monetarily the sport is very heavy and it needs

00:13:03.600 --> 00:13:06.880
you to be a bit more stable in your life before you can start

00:13:06.880 --> 00:13:09.800
going into it. But I'm like, if there is a late

00:13:09.800 --> 00:13:13.840
teen, preteen or early 20s pilot listening to this and they want

00:13:13.840 --> 00:13:18.120
to go out and chase the glory as hard as possible, what would be

00:13:18.120 --> 00:13:22.320
the ideal body type, training type and fuelling?

00:13:22.320 --> 00:13:25.680
Because if you as a researcher who has all of these resources

00:13:25.680 --> 00:13:29.480
is saying the data set is fairly small, then these people who

00:13:29.480 --> 00:13:33.320
just have a dream or with a vision are basically layman and

00:13:33.320 --> 00:13:36.960
their only reference points is the small bits and pieces of

00:13:36.960 --> 00:13:39.400
media that gets put out in short form or long form.

00:13:39.760 --> 00:13:43.000
So I feel like, and that was one of the reason why I started the

00:13:43.000 --> 00:13:46.040
show actually, because I wanted, I had so many questions which I

00:13:46.040 --> 00:13:47.680
just could not find an answer to.

00:13:48.080 --> 00:13:53.080
And that is why I'm trying to figure out if we can give our

00:13:53.080 --> 00:13:55.800
audiences, I wouldn't say a mantra of some sort, but some

00:13:55.800 --> 00:13:58.440
sort of a baseline that all right, if you want to make it

00:13:58.520 --> 00:14:02.600
large or do it in the most professional way, then this is

00:14:02.600 --> 00:14:05.640
where you should aim for when it comes to performing physically.

00:14:06.520 --> 00:14:07.600
Yeah, no, I see. Exactly.

00:14:07.600 --> 00:14:10.280
See where you're coming from. That quote from Russ Ogden is a

00:14:10.280 --> 00:14:12.680
shame because I started paragliding a week after.

00:14:16.920 --> 00:14:19.960
My first, I'm going to have him on the show soon, you know, so

00:14:20.200 --> 00:14:25.240
we talked about that, so. I think it's interesting, isn't

00:14:25.240 --> 00:14:26.480
it? Is the question, is there

00:14:26.480 --> 00:14:31.880
something that is inherently important about being young,

00:14:31.960 --> 00:14:35.200
being in your teens and 20s? Or is it simply that just gives

