I think Physiology is a relatively small component.

00:15:16.920 --> 00:15:21.920
I think my view on it anyway is that what is probably going to

00:15:21.920 --> 00:15:25.920
get people the furthest is consistent goal setting.

00:15:26.600 --> 00:15:30.680
And Malin Log at Flyyo has done some great stuff around this,

00:15:30.680 --> 00:15:34.040
really effectively setting goals and working out ways to achieve

00:15:34.040 --> 00:15:37.840
them. And also I think the sports

00:15:37.840 --> 00:15:41.520
psychology side of things, Kriegel started winning when he

00:15:41.520 --> 00:15:44.760
started working with sports psychologists.

00:15:45.800 --> 00:15:53.800
Because once I think you've mastered the basics of pulling

00:15:53.800 --> 00:15:57.480
the strings and once you've got better at thermaling and

00:15:57.480 --> 00:16:01.840
gliding, the psychological component becomes so huge that I

00:16:01.840 --> 00:16:04.360
think starting that early on and developing very good

00:16:04.360 --> 00:16:08.760
psychological habits is probably an important route to success.

00:16:10.080 --> 00:16:13.040
In terms of the Physiology stuff though, purely I would think of

00:16:13.040 --> 00:16:18.800
it in terms of margin. If I think about my worst days

00:16:18.800 --> 00:16:23.600
in the sky, there are ones where I've exhausted myself on the way

00:16:23.600 --> 00:16:26.720
up to launch because in Scotland we've got to walk a lot.

00:16:27.840 --> 00:16:33.400
I'm dehydrated, I'm tired, I'm worried, I'm out of currency,

00:16:34.600 --> 00:16:38.800
the conditions are marginal. Those are the things that I

00:16:38.800 --> 00:16:43.040
guess would set me up for failure as opposed to success.

00:16:43.880 --> 00:16:47.960
So when we think about how we might flip that around in terms

00:16:47.960 --> 00:16:53.320
of performance, you think, OK, you need enough general fitness

00:16:53.520 --> 00:16:56.920
that the act of getting to launch and getting ready is not

00:16:56.920 --> 00:17:00.680
going to be an exhausting or barrier to your day.

00:17:01.240 --> 00:17:04.240
You need the right kit. So because that helps you

00:17:04.240 --> 00:17:07.359
getting up there, getting set up, you need to make sure you're

00:17:07.359 --> 00:17:09.440
sleeping well. You need to make sure you're

00:17:09.440 --> 00:17:12.839
well fed and hydrated, as we've discussed, need to make sure

00:17:12.839 --> 00:17:16.000
that you've not been drinking or smashing a load of drugs the

00:17:16.000 --> 00:17:19.200
night before such that your sleep has been disrupted and

00:17:19.200 --> 00:17:21.319
you're hungover. You need to think about things

00:17:21.319 --> 00:17:23.640
like sun protection, cold protection.

00:17:24.800 --> 00:17:29.920
All of those things, I guess are about giving you margin to fly

00:17:29.920 --> 00:17:32.720
your paraglider well and to make those good decisions.

00:17:34.440 --> 00:17:36.920
OK. I just quickly want to touch up

00:17:36.920 --> 00:17:41.120
on the topic of ideal body type and I think as a reference

00:17:41.120 --> 00:17:44.080
points we can take racing paragliders in a World Cup

00:17:44.080 --> 00:17:47.360
because obviously we can't just have all the other scenarios

00:17:47.360 --> 00:17:50.800
where you are a beacon flyer or you're going on a trip or like

00:17:50.800 --> 00:17:54.200
the acro competition. So let's say you are a pilot who

00:17:54.200 --> 00:17:59.360
is racing in a cross country World Cup and ideal body type.

00:18:00.080 --> 00:18:02.480
Of course, the glider sizes, the ballast and everything is a

00:18:02.480 --> 00:18:03.600
whole different story. Which.

00:18:03.840 --> 00:18:05.920
I was going to say, I think the ideal body type is just being an

00:18:05.920 --> 00:18:10.240
absolute unit, isn't? It I think a lot of us saw that

00:18:10.240 --> 00:18:14.320
meme slash photo which Baptiste posted when they put a limit on

00:18:14.320 --> 00:18:18.040
the ballast that you cannot ballast more than 32 kilograms.

00:18:18.040 --> 00:18:21.600
And Baptiste posted a photo of him Maxime and Honor and with

00:18:21.600 --> 00:18:25.240
like huge ass protruding bellies coming out and saying that is

00:18:25.240 --> 00:18:27.440
how futurist leads are going to look like.

00:18:28.600 --> 00:18:31.960
I don't know because Formula One, yes you do lose a lot of

00:18:31.960 --> 00:18:34.520
water or 5 kilograms or something like that.

00:18:34.520 --> 00:18:39.000
And I per race and I remember Horan saying that he also loses

00:18:39.040 --> 00:18:40.800
up to a few kilogram after a race.

00:18:41.240 --> 00:18:46.680
I'm just wondering, is an ideal body type someone like Horan or

00:18:46.760 --> 00:18:50.320
can they be a bit more muscular because of the amount of G

00:18:50.320 --> 00:18:54.560
forces that we go through? Or can there be a more stronger

00:18:54.560 --> 00:18:58.440
core because of the amount of the the core usage in, in those

00:18:58.440 --> 00:19:00.920
kind of scenarios? And of course, in our pre show

00:19:00.920 --> 00:19:04.600
notes, we exchanged the lower like benefits or lower heart

00:19:04.600 --> 00:19:07.840
rate and like avoiding the heart rate peak surges during the

00:19:07.840 --> 00:19:09.040
stress periods or something like that.

00:19:09.280 --> 00:19:11.440
So what? What's your take on that?

00:19:12.360 --> 00:19:16.400
I think one of the nice things about paragliding is that it's

00:19:16.560 --> 00:19:19.760
pretty democratic in terms of the body types that it will

00:19:19.760 --> 00:19:22.440
accept compared to something like the high jump.

00:19:22.440 --> 00:19:30.280
I guess I think all of the points you allude to are things

00:19:30.280 --> 00:19:33.480
that could be helpful under certain circumstances.

00:19:33.480 --> 00:19:37.680
So having being someone who naturally has more G tolerance,

00:19:37.720 --> 00:19:40.280
which has a little bit to do with the heart reflexes, a

00:19:40.280 --> 00:19:45.120
little bit to do with the gap between heart and brain, and

00:19:45.320 --> 00:19:47.360
also a little bit to do, as you say with musculature.

00:19:48.200 --> 00:19:50.760
That's something that might be helpful if you have to do a

00:19:50.760 --> 00:19:53.240
spiral dive, but it's also something that's very trainable

00:19:53.680 --> 00:19:56.240
and it's also something that's overcome able through equipment.

00:19:56.240 --> 00:19:57.920
So for example, like a drogue sheet.

00:19:58.760 --> 00:20:06.920
The the other stuff I think is probably just dwarfed by size if

00:20:06.920 --> 00:20:10.040
you're really talking about competition and your ability to

00:20:10.040 --> 00:20:13.520
fly the largest possible glider for all the aerodynamic reasons

00:20:13.520 --> 00:20:16.800
that we know. Beyond that, I don't think there

00:20:16.800 --> 00:20:20.400
is an ideal body type and I think that's a great thing.

00:20:20.400 --> 00:20:22.400
I think that's something to actually to be celebrated about

00:20:22.560 --> 00:20:27.040
our sport. And I personally would love to

00:20:27.040 --> 00:20:31.040
see rule changes in competition, which I know are difficult to

00:20:31.040 --> 00:20:32.520
do. And I know lots of people have

00:20:32.520 --> 00:20:34.800
tried it through the wait lists or through the pool needle

00:20:34.800 --> 00:20:40.080
discussion or all of these things such that irrespective of

00:20:40.080 --> 00:20:43.320
your size, you can compete on a level playing field.

00:20:43.920 --> 00:20:50.240
And also I think that having to carry ballast is one of the most

00:20:50.760 --> 00:20:55.800
dangerous and unnecessary things that we do because the

00:20:55.800 --> 00:20:58.320
artificiality of what we have created.

00:20:58.320 --> 00:21:01.400
If we think about paragliding generally, like most people

00:21:01.400 --> 00:21:03.440
think, if you say paragliding, it's that thing where they pull

00:21:03.440 --> 00:21:07.840
you behind the boat. So no one really knows what it

00:21:07.840 --> 00:21:10.240
is we do. And lots of people in

00:21:10.240 --> 00:21:14.480
paragliding don't know who the world champion is, let alone my

00:21:14.480 --> 00:21:18.920
mother. So I think we've got this really

00:21:18.920 --> 00:21:21.760
niche thing. And so we have the opportunity

00:21:21.760 --> 00:21:24.960
to make this really niche thing what we want it to be.

00:21:25.720 --> 00:21:32.000
And so creating an environment where if you want to race at the

00:21:32.000 --> 00:21:36.560
highest level, you have to fly a harness that is uncredibly

00:21:36.560 --> 00:21:39.960
uncomfortable, makes you sweat, the tail wraps around your face

00:21:39.960 --> 00:21:44.400
when you stall, that if you're a small pilot, you have to carry

00:21:44.400 --> 00:21:48.280
30kg of ballast. That is a genuine health hazard,

00:21:48.520 --> 00:21:51.240
I would say on take off and on landing.

00:21:52.280 --> 00:21:56.520
To me that feels just put it kindly, like a wasted

00:21:56.520 --> 00:21:58.840
opportunity. There are ways of putting it

00:21:58.840 --> 00:22:01.800
less kindly. So I personally would love to

00:22:01.800 --> 00:22:09.200
see us work together as a group to make it I guess both more

00:22:09.440 --> 00:22:11.880
more inclusive and also just more enjoyable for everyone

00:22:11.880 --> 00:22:13.760
involved. Because as you say, there are so

00:22:13.760 --> 00:22:18.600
few professional pilots that for most of us this is our hobby.

00:22:19.200 --> 00:22:24.120
Why are we making competing at our hobby actively unpleasant

00:22:24.280 --> 00:22:27.640
for quite a large proportion of the people that do it, and

00:22:27.640 --> 00:22:30.240
actively risky? But I'm also not a very

00:22:30.240 --> 00:22:34.280
competitive person, so I accept that other people have different

00:22:34.280 --> 00:22:37.400
views on that. Yeah, of course when it comes to

00:22:37.400 --> 00:22:41.360
competing, there is a certain aspect of our sport of pilots

00:22:41.360 --> 00:22:44.800
which are like, yes, the more crazier, the better, the more

00:22:44.800 --> 00:22:46.960
fun. And then there's a rational

00:22:46.960 --> 00:22:48.680
side, which is let's be obvious guys.

00:22:49.120 --> 00:22:53.880
Well, I quickly want to touch upon the environmental side of

00:22:53.880 --> 00:22:55.840
the sport, that of course there's one thing that you can

00:22:55.840 --> 00:22:59.800
feel right that you can have a certain like controllable, such

00:22:59.800 --> 00:23:02.200
as the equipment and the laws and the rules and everything

00:23:02.200 --> 00:23:04.600
which kind of make it like that. But then there's a certain bit

00:23:04.600 --> 00:23:07.520
of, what do you say? Like what nature throws at us.

00:23:07.920 --> 00:23:13.040
And a lot of times you are flying in situations which are

00:23:13.040 --> 00:23:15.040
new, which are out of the ordinary.

00:23:15.400 --> 00:23:21.400
And usually one thing that I can say is that wind factor is such

00:23:21.400 --> 00:23:24.520
a huge thing in paragliding that it it surprises you more often

00:23:24.520 --> 00:23:26.320
than not. And there have been so many

00:23:26.320 --> 00:23:28.560
times that I've gone up in the air and I've ended up with

00:23:28.560 --> 00:23:31.160
frosties on my fingers and I've had to like leave the risers and

00:23:31.160 --> 00:23:33.040
bring them back and warm them up.

00:23:33.320 --> 00:23:36.840
So when it comes to dealing with the environment, what is the

00:23:36.840 --> 00:23:41.560
biggest risk that you can be exposed to if you're not very

00:23:41.560 --> 00:23:44.160
well prepared for it? Because being prepared for

00:23:44.160 --> 00:23:46.920
something like that is only experience and experience comes

00:23:46.920 --> 00:23:50.800
from trying and getting to know about things.

00:23:50.800 --> 00:23:54.440
So it's, there's no ideal way to go out and saying that, yes,

00:23:54.480 --> 00:23:57.040
this is how I will do it unless you've actually done it a few

00:23:57.040 --> 00:23:58.240
times. And then they're like, no, OK, I

00:23:58.240 --> 00:24:02.000
need to fine tune it a bit. So I totally agree with you and

00:24:02.560 --> 00:24:05.680
many people would say this because it's my passion or my

00:24:05.680 --> 00:24:08.200
profession, but don't underestimate these

00:24:08.200 --> 00:24:11.320
environmental factors. I think we have a tendency to do

00:24:11.320 --> 00:24:14.840
that. If we think about the

00:24:14.840 --> 00:24:18.760
environmental factors that can affect us, they're really oxygen

00:24:18.760 --> 00:24:24.560
level, cold G force and sun exposure.

00:24:24.880 --> 00:24:26.520
I would say like those are the main ones.

00:24:26.960 --> 00:24:35.360
The oxygen thing is interesting because I've flown a lot in

00:24:35.360 --> 00:24:37.200
India. It's my favorite place to fly.

00:24:37.320 --> 00:24:40.400
I was fortunate to fly in Pakistan a few years ago, and

00:24:40.400 --> 00:24:43.800
that was wonderful as well, and to go up nice and high.

00:24:46.040 --> 00:24:49.440
Undoubtedly lower levels of oxygen affect us.

00:24:49.520 --> 00:24:51.920
They affect our ability to think.

00:24:52.080 --> 00:24:55.320
They affect, in some cases, if it gets low enough, our ability

00:24:55.320 --> 00:24:58.240
to see effectively in terms of our peripheral vision.

00:24:58.280 --> 00:25:01.000
All of those things narrow. They affect our ability to

00:25:01.000 --> 00:25:05.760
control the glider. And they also have a bit of a

00:25:05.760 --> 00:25:10.280
hangover effect. So if you get hypoxic and then

00:25:10.280 --> 00:25:13.360
you go down to a low altitude, you feel better, but you're

00:25:13.360 --> 00:25:17.240
still not back to 100% for probably up to about 90 minutes.

00:25:17.400 --> 00:25:19.000
For those wondering what's hypoxic?

00:25:20.760 --> 00:25:24.080
So hypoxia is low levels of oxygen in the blood.

00:25:24.560 --> 00:25:26.280
Technically the term for that is hypoxemia.

00:25:26.800 --> 00:25:31.440
But I would think about it as we're in an environment when we

00:25:31.440 --> 00:25:33.280
fly high where the pressure is low.

00:25:33.680 --> 00:25:36.680
That means that the oxygen molecules, there's still 20%

00:25:36.680 --> 00:25:40.120
oxygen, same as there is at sea level, but they're all further

00:25:40.120 --> 00:25:43.680
apart. So that means to get the same

00:25:43.680 --> 00:25:46.920
amount in, you have to breathe faster, which is why actually

00:25:46.920 --> 00:25:49.760
flying at house 2 is so dehydrating, because what's

00:25:49.760 --> 00:25:52.440
happening is you're breathing in and out lots and you're shedding

00:25:52.800 --> 00:25:56.480
water each time you do so that that's one of the reasons why

00:25:56.480 --> 00:25:58.680
it's dehydrating. The other reason is because the

00:25:58.680 --> 00:26:01.520
oxygen levels are lower. Your kidneys don't work as well,

00:26:01.720 --> 00:26:04.160
so you don't conserve water in the same way, which is why you

00:26:04.160 --> 00:26:07.800
desperately need to pee when you fly at high altitude or in cold

00:26:07.800 --> 00:26:12.040
environments. So oxygen's definitely a thing.

00:26:12.840 --> 00:26:16.720
It's hard to know what height it starts to matter at because that

00:26:16.720 --> 00:26:21.640
depends quite a lot on different people and it also depends on

00:26:21.880 --> 00:26:25.560
and by different people. Climatisation, sorry, genetics

00:26:25.560 --> 00:26:27.600
and fitness. It also depends on

00:26:27.600 --> 00:26:29.920
acclimatisation. We talk about that in a SEC.

00:26:31.000 --> 00:26:36.280
The there's a very easy way to fix it, which is to carry

00:26:36.280 --> 00:26:39.880
supplementary oxygen. But there's also a lot of

00:26:39.880 --> 00:26:43.920
associated faff that comes with carrying supplementary oxygen.

00:26:44.520 --> 00:26:47.840
But I can only say from personal experience that when I flew

00:26:47.840 --> 00:26:51.840
within Pakistan, when I switched it on, suddenly my mood

00:26:51.840 --> 00:26:53.200
improved. I felt warmer.

00:26:53.200 --> 00:26:55.920
I could think more clearly. I almost felt like I could.

00:26:56.720 --> 00:26:58.440
I don't know if I could physically see better.

00:26:58.440 --> 00:27:02.560
I just felt less blinkered. But I felt a striking increase

00:27:02.560 --> 00:27:07.200
in bandwidth. So it's changed my perception a

00:27:07.200 --> 00:27:10.240
little bit in terms of I knew academically that hypoxia was an

00:27:10.240 --> 00:27:12.480
issue. It's first, it takes a while to

00:27:12.480 --> 00:27:14.960
really feel it, and then when you feel it, you're like OK.

00:27:14.960 --> 00:27:18.160
Or when you feel it going away or improving with oxygen, it

00:27:18.160 --> 00:27:20.080
strikes you how much it might be affecting you.

00:27:20.560 --> 00:27:23.160
What are the first symptoms that come to you that you can

00:27:23.160 --> 00:27:24.960
understand? Because as we spoke about in the

00:27:24.960 --> 00:27:27.760
beginning of the show, that it's such a cognitively heavy load

00:27:27.800 --> 00:27:32.080
and there's so much to look and observe and be constantly

00:27:32.080 --> 00:27:34.160
thinking that it's so easy to be drifted away.

00:27:34.440 --> 00:27:37.680
So how do you identify that this might be coming in the next

00:27:37.680 --> 00:27:40.880
10-15 minutes? This is why your point about

00:27:40.880 --> 00:27:46.120
experience is such a good one because everybody has their own.

00:27:46.120 --> 00:27:49.280
It's called a hypoxic signature. And so if you're a fighter

00:27:49.280 --> 00:27:55.520
pilot, you get put in a chamber, you get made hypoxic, and then

00:27:55.520 --> 00:27:57.480
you get a load of puzzles and other things to do.

00:27:57.480 --> 00:27:59.120
And there's some brilliant videos of all these, like

00:27:59.120 --> 00:28:02.920
incredibly macho U.S. Marine pilots, just like mashing

00:28:03.240 --> 00:28:05.520
like kids toys together. It's fun to watch.

00:28:05.880 --> 00:28:09.280
But the purpose of that training for them is both to realise that

00:28:09.280 --> 00:28:12.840
they don't know they're impaired, but also to try and

00:28:12.840 --> 00:28:15.480
work out exactly what you said, like how did they feel in the

00:28:16.280 --> 00:28:18.160
the time leading up to that impairment?

00:28:18.160 --> 00:28:22.440
Because it varies. And so finding our own hypoxic

00:28:22.440 --> 00:28:24.760
signature as paradelight of pilots is a little bit difficult

00:28:24.760 --> 00:28:26.600
because we don't have access to these chambers.

00:28:27.080 --> 00:28:32.640
But I think one of the way to do it is to really try and be

00:28:32.640 --> 00:28:36.080
observant of yourself when you go into these environments.

00:28:36.520 --> 00:28:40.880
So if say I'm in beer, I'm going over the back and flying up

00:28:40.880 --> 00:28:44.840
above 4000 meters. I'm asking myself what am I

00:28:44.840 --> 00:28:46.920
feeling? What can I see, what kind of

00:28:46.920 --> 00:28:48.280
thing? And actually, because I was

00:28:48.280 --> 00:28:51.400
quite interested in this, I put a little note on my cockpit to

00:28:51.400 --> 00:28:53.760
remind me to think about what I'm thinking.

00:28:54.080 --> 00:28:56.640
Because you can get so in the zone.

00:28:57.360 --> 00:29:00.480
But typically for people, they would say things like mood

00:29:00.480 --> 00:29:02.480
changes. So you either feel pretty

00:29:02.480 --> 00:29:06.320
euphoric or you feel a bit sad or a bit constrained or a bit

00:29:06.320 --> 00:29:08.800
afraid. Not having as big a bandwidth,

00:29:09.000 --> 00:29:12.400
some changes in motor skills, so you're not thermaling as nicely

00:29:12.400 --> 00:29:14.600
as you would normally. You're bouncing in and out.

00:29:15.240 --> 00:29:19.240
Then as you get more hypoxic, the way you communicate with

00:29:19.240 --> 00:29:22.480
others might be a thing. So sometimes people become less

00:29:22.480 --> 00:29:24.720
commutative, sometimes that become quite garrulous,

00:29:24.720 --> 00:29:27.120
sometimes they start talking a bit of nonsense.

00:29:27.560 --> 00:29:29.760
Certainly if that's happening, it's too late.

00:29:30.120 --> 00:29:32.360
But that's also the advantage of flying with buddies because they

00:29:32.360 --> 00:29:35.080
can point out to you that this thing is happening.

00:29:36.040 --> 00:29:38.600
Obviously, the most kind of telling sign that it might be

00:29:38.600 --> 00:29:40.440
happening is just the fact that you are high.

00:29:40.800 --> 00:29:44.360
So taking a note of your altitudes and think that OK, I'm

00:29:44.360 --> 00:29:49.440
now entering A vulnerable place and I would say probably about

00:29:49.760 --> 00:29:52.000
for most people because this varies a lot between

00:29:52.000 --> 00:29:55.040
individuals. Once you start flying above 3000

00:29:55.040 --> 00:29:58.880
meters, certainly 3 1/2, you are going to be subject to this.

00:29:59.160 --> 00:30:03.440
All right, let's say how many minutes above 3000 because a lot

00:30:03.440 --> 00:30:05.720
of times we are, I wouldn't say exactly dolphining.

00:30:05.720 --> 00:30:07.920
You're getting high, lower, high, lower depending on the

00:30:07.920 --> 00:30:11.560
glides. So when do you start hitting the

00:30:11.560 --> 00:30:15.200
point of no return that OK, now I'm pushing my limits and then

00:30:15.560 --> 00:30:17.680
high box, yeah, is a very real thing now because I've been

00:30:17.680 --> 00:30:19.120
doing this for like past two hours.

00:30:20.560 --> 00:30:23.920
So again this no evidence specific to paragliders, but

00:30:23.920 --> 00:30:29.120
that I guess the aviation like general aviation would be above

00:30:29.120 --> 00:30:35.040
10,000 feet for 30 minutes or above 13,000 feet for any period

00:30:35.040 --> 00:30:39.120
of time. So I don't know what 10,000 feet

00:30:39.120 --> 00:30:42.320
is that about 2802 thousand 600 meters, something like that.

00:30:42.560 --> 00:30:45.120
But that's the general aviation guidance.

