00:46:11.680 --> 00:46:14.760
the lung. Now interestingly doesn't seem

00:46:14.760 --> 00:46:17.920
to work. Variants on Viagra are still in

00:46:17.920 --> 00:46:20.440
some of the guidelines and the American guidelines in

00:46:20.440 --> 00:46:23.640
particular, but really not that helpful for something that

00:46:23.640 --> 00:46:26.160
should be textbook perfect for altitude.

00:46:26.960 --> 00:46:31.880
But this, and I can't tell you why, but this experience for me

00:46:31.880 --> 00:46:34.840
was very eye opening because I realised one, altitude is

00:46:34.840 --> 00:46:41.880
fascinating and two, there's some semblance of a career that

00:46:41.880 --> 00:46:46.040
can be made within medicine and research where you get to go

00:46:46.040 --> 00:46:49.640
outside and climb mountains. And so that was what kind of set

00:46:49.640 --> 00:46:54.160
me off down this path. And I've been lucky enough my

00:46:54.160 --> 00:46:57.680
kind of core specialty is anesthesia and critical care

00:46:57.680 --> 00:47:01.400
medicine, but I've been lucky enough to spend more time out of

00:47:01.400 --> 00:47:05.160
the hospital than in working up at high altitude, particularly

00:47:05.160 --> 00:47:10.360
in Nepal, a little bit in India. And so it's great because it

00:47:10.360 --> 00:47:13.000
means that all the stuff I read about and doing the lab, you get

00:47:13.000 --> 00:47:16.120
to see and some of the lessons I can bring back to paragliding.

00:47:16.840 --> 00:47:18.840
COVID kind of killed a lot of that for me.

00:47:19.400 --> 00:47:22.880
So it was all going quite well until February 2020 when I

00:47:22.880 --> 00:47:24.440
thought, oh, I don't have to go back to the hospital.

00:47:24.560 --> 00:47:27.520
I have enough. This altitude expedition will

00:47:27.560 --> 00:47:31.320
feed and clothe me. That came to a crashing halt in

00:47:31.320 --> 00:47:33.680
March 2020. I was very lucky.

00:47:33.680 --> 00:47:35.720
I got to go back to work in my local hospital.

00:47:35.720 --> 00:47:39.800
So I did that for wave 1 of COVID and then now I work online

00:47:39.800 --> 00:47:44.400
for a remote care company and that at least has the advantage

00:47:44.440 --> 00:47:48.680
of being very flexible in terms of where I work my hours, which

00:47:48.680 --> 00:47:52.120
is great for going flying and for travelling around the world

00:47:52.120 --> 00:47:54.080
to some of the lovely spots where we go flying.

00:47:54.520 --> 00:47:59.000
I still keep one day a week where I do altitude and cold

00:47:59.000 --> 00:48:01.400
research through the Extreme Environments Lab at the

00:48:01.400 --> 00:48:03.600
University of Portsmouth, and that's also where Jess is

00:48:03.600 --> 00:48:06.760
enrolled as a student. So I used to do it all the time.

00:48:07.840 --> 00:48:11.160
Someone ate a bat, caused COVID, ruined my life.

00:48:11.280 --> 00:48:15.480
But I've fallen on my feet and I now get to visit these places as

00:48:15.480 --> 00:48:17.320
a tourist and still do a little bit of research.

00:48:17.920 --> 00:48:21.120
It sounds fancy to say that I'm a tourist at Extreme

00:48:21.120 --> 00:48:23.480
Environments Lab. The name itself sounds so cool.

00:48:25.040 --> 00:48:26.560
It's a great place. They do amazing stuff.

00:48:26.560 --> 00:48:28.480
Actually. There are cold water labs.

00:48:28.480 --> 00:48:31.800
So they grew out of the Institute of Naval Medicine and

00:48:31.800 --> 00:48:35.400
they've done an enormous amount of work around why young people

00:48:35.400 --> 00:48:37.240
drown. They've really, that's the kind

00:48:37.240 --> 00:48:41.000
of core specialty. And then I came and just as they

00:48:41.000 --> 00:48:44.880
were expanding from a cold water lab to a lab that focuses on

00:48:44.880 --> 00:48:48.600
lots of different stressors. So things like epoxy, a cold

00:48:48.880 --> 00:48:52.320
heat, sleep deprivation, and how they will interact.

00:48:52.840 --> 00:48:55.560
And then I came along and said we should look at paragliding.

00:48:55.560 --> 00:48:57.880
And so I was able to do a little bit of work through them on the

00:48:57.880 --> 00:49:00.280
paragliding and then I helped support all the other more

00:49:00.280 --> 00:49:02.080
general stuff. So what's the paragliding side

00:49:02.080 --> 00:49:04.880
of the lab that you? So the paragliding side, the

00:49:04.880 --> 00:49:06.360
lab, we've done a few things through that.

00:49:06.400 --> 00:49:11.960
We've created a simulator where we got a paragliding harness,

00:49:12.280 --> 00:49:16.280
put it in an environmental chamber and created hypoxia,

00:49:16.280 --> 00:49:21.520
cold and headland. And we got pilots to do a

00:49:21.520 --> 00:49:26.680
simulated flight up to 3 1/2 thousand meters and do cognitive

00:49:26.680 --> 00:49:31.040
tests. And what I found from that was a

00:49:31.040 --> 00:49:34.240
complete muddle, which comes back to what I said at the

00:49:34.240 --> 00:49:37.760
beginning, which is that it was a really small group of people.

00:49:38.360 --> 00:49:42.960
And sure, like when they got up high, they were less good at the

00:49:42.960 --> 00:49:45.440
cognitive tests. Their reaction time changed.

00:49:45.440 --> 00:49:47.440
They weren't very good at stacking blocks and all the

00:49:47.440 --> 00:49:51.480
things that we had them do. But what that told me about how

00:49:51.480 --> 00:49:55.880
well somebody would thermal or whether they'd fly into the

00:49:55.880 --> 00:50:00.240
Cliff or not, I think it's impossible to say.

00:50:00.360 --> 00:50:06.480
And that was as much kind of my growth as a researcher going

00:50:06.480 --> 00:50:09.600
through that process is anything that contributed to paragliding.

00:50:09.920 --> 00:50:14.280
But what came out of it was this realisation that if we want to

00:50:14.280 --> 00:50:19.480
learn stuff in paragliding, we need to focus on really specific

00:50:19.480 --> 00:50:21.000
things. Like what I've done in that

00:50:21.000 --> 00:50:25.560
chamber was too general. And So what I then did next was

00:50:25.560 --> 00:50:30.480
say, OK, the thing that kills paragliders is hitting the

00:50:30.480 --> 00:50:33.760
ground. What stops paraglide is hitting

00:50:33.760 --> 00:50:35.960
the ground. The reserve parachute is

00:50:35.960 --> 00:50:37.560
probably the last line of defence.

00:50:38.720 --> 00:50:41.040
Why don't people throw the reserve parachute?

00:50:41.840 --> 00:50:45.680
And so then I started doing the work that I think has been most

00:50:45.680 --> 00:50:49.880
useful for paragliding, which was around reserves, how we need

00:50:49.880 --> 00:50:52.320
to be different, why people do or don't throw them.

00:50:54.120 --> 00:50:56.720
Fascinating. I'm very tempted to ask, could

00:50:56.720 --> 00:50:59.760
you please tell us what that research was about and what have

00:50:59.760 --> 00:51:03.320
the findings been so far? Yeah, sure.

00:51:03.360 --> 00:51:09.920
We did two things. We got 55 pilots and took them

00:51:09.920 --> 00:51:14.880
to a zip line and we put them on a zip line and we got them to do

00:51:15.080 --> 00:51:17.960
a cognitive challenge. So they looked up at 2 flashing

00:51:17.960 --> 00:51:19.600
lights. They had to pull their brake

00:51:19.600 --> 00:51:23.560
lines along with kind of flashing lights, and we got them

00:51:23.560 --> 00:51:27.440
to say all the things that could come up, beginning with a

00:51:27.440 --> 00:51:30.040
certain letter. The idea being that the hands

00:51:30.040 --> 00:51:32.240
were engaged, their eyes were in a position as if they were

00:51:32.240 --> 00:51:35.480
fixing a wing, and we'd give them a cognitive challenge.

00:51:36.600 --> 00:51:40.840
Then they were released down the zip line and they had to throw

00:51:40.840 --> 00:51:44.480
their reserve parachute as fast as possible, so they had to make

00:51:44.480 --> 00:51:49.840
a cognitive switch. We then from that work, we then

00:51:49.840 --> 00:51:54.760
went to the G Force simulator at Frutschler Hocheries in German,

00:51:54.760 --> 00:51:57.920
who were wonderful people. Jess Barlow there, who ran at

00:51:57.920 --> 00:52:01.160
the time, was so helpful. And we repeated the same model

00:52:01.360 --> 00:52:05.480
with 83 pilots, but this time they're in rotation forwards and

00:52:05.480 --> 00:52:08.200
backwards between sort of two and 4G.

00:52:09.160 --> 00:52:13.520
And the reason why I think this was useful work was because for

00:52:13.520 --> 00:52:17.200
once in one of these studies, we had enough people and we were

00:52:17.200 --> 00:52:19.240
looking at something really specific.

00:52:20.280 --> 00:52:25.760
And So what we found was that, first of all, pilots look for

00:52:25.760 --> 00:52:30.040
their reserve handle along their skeleton.

00:52:30.640 --> 00:52:33.920
So if they're rotating forwards, their hand naturally goes to

00:52:33.920 --> 00:52:36.800
their hip bone. If they're rotating backwards,

00:52:36.800 --> 00:52:39.880
as they would in a sat or an auto rotation, they go a bit

00:52:40.000 --> 00:52:43.360
further down their thigh. But when the reserve handle

00:52:43.360 --> 00:52:47.600
wasn't in those places, you could see people just repeatedly

00:52:47.600 --> 00:52:50.240
tapping all around trying to find it.

00:52:51.480 --> 00:52:57.200
We also found that pilots would naturally pull up on the reserve

00:52:57.200 --> 00:53:01.360
handle even if the reserve was designed to be pulled outwards.

00:53:02.000 --> 00:53:04.920
We found that they needed to grip it in a particular way.

00:53:05.360 --> 00:53:10.640
And all of these things gave us design suggestions for how a

00:53:10.640 --> 00:53:15.240
reserve handle should be because what we found in the studies

00:53:15.240 --> 00:53:20.360
consistently is that when people had a problem, they couldn't

00:53:20.360 --> 00:53:24.280
innovate, like they couldn't fix it under stress.

00:53:24.880 --> 00:53:28.680
So that to me means that our kit has to work for us.

00:53:29.480 --> 00:53:32.480
We can't expect someone to. If you tell somebody what you

00:53:32.480 --> 00:53:35.480
need to do, if you're need to throw your reserve as you need

00:53:35.480 --> 00:53:38.280
to put yourself into Batfly, you need to find your reserve

00:53:38.280 --> 00:53:40.000
handle, you need to throw it towards your feet.

00:53:40.000 --> 00:53:43.840
All the stuff that people say, all of that's true, I'm sure,

00:53:44.080 --> 00:53:47.840
but the average person can't do that even in a simulated

00:53:47.840 --> 00:53:50.120
environment. There's just so much going on

00:53:50.320 --> 00:53:52.280
that we need to make it easy for people.

00:53:53.120 --> 00:53:56.120
And I would say there's been like mixed uptake from the

00:53:56.120 --> 00:53:57.960
research. Like I made some videos that

00:53:57.960 --> 00:54:00.960
show all of this online, can send you the link, wrote to the

00:54:00.960 --> 00:54:03.760
manufacturers, wrote to the EM committee.

00:54:04.600 --> 00:54:07.920
They adopted some of the recommendations and some of the

00:54:08.240 --> 00:54:11.040
new harnesses like supper. The supper ones were great.

00:54:11.360 --> 00:54:14.600
The new, it might be the advanced one.

00:54:14.600 --> 00:54:16.120
The new advanced one is beautiful.

00:54:16.120 --> 00:54:19.760
It has a really nice big chunky handle.

00:54:19.760 --> 00:54:22.600
It's just on the hip. All of this stuff is wonderful.

00:54:23.000 --> 00:54:27.640
The little tiny strings that you see on some of them are clearly

00:54:27.880 --> 00:54:31.720
less in tune with what the average pilot does.

00:54:32.320 --> 00:54:34.880
But we've made some progress and particularly reserve containers,

00:54:34.880 --> 00:54:37.400
like most reserves now, you can pull them out at any angle.

00:54:37.920 --> 00:54:41.040
So I really like that work because I thought it was robust,

00:54:41.320 --> 00:54:43.840
it was repeatable. We did it on lots of people.

00:54:44.040 --> 00:54:46.520
We came up with recommendations that were concrete and that we

00:54:46.520 --> 00:54:48.520
could change to make our sport better.

00:54:49.160 --> 00:54:53.240
So the Chamber study at Portsmouth was good to do

00:54:53.240 --> 00:54:56.280
because it led to this stuff. I wouldn't necessarily take all

00:54:56.280 --> 00:54:57.960
that I found from that and base your life.

00:54:58.080 --> 00:55:04.360
The question that comes to our mind is that when like XR7 EXO

00:55:04.360 --> 00:55:09.280
seed, every other reserve handle is generally at your hip, and

00:55:09.280 --> 00:55:12.000
then suddenly we move to submarine style where it's a

00:55:12.000 --> 00:55:14.080
game changer. It's right in front of you.

00:55:14.680 --> 00:55:19.480
So there is definitely a visual sense sensory input that is

00:55:19.480 --> 00:55:21.720
coming to you that is saying that, hey, there it is.

00:55:22.280 --> 00:55:25.120
So no matter how much you're panicking, it's very hard to

00:55:25.120 --> 00:55:28.600
miss out. But as a pilot or as an end

00:55:28.600 --> 00:55:35.920
user, when you're switching your equipment, is it worth it?

00:55:35.920 --> 00:55:37.760
Or is it not worth risking your life?

00:55:39.680 --> 00:55:42.760
That's such a good question and I love that you came to that as

00:55:42.760 --> 00:55:45.920
well because when I went into the reserve studies, I was like

00:55:45.920 --> 00:55:48.360
front mount is going to be better, obviously going to be

00:55:48.360 --> 00:55:50.480
better. It's right in front, but what we

00:55:50.480 --> 00:55:53.720
found was that there was no difference in the time it took

00:55:53.720 --> 00:55:58.600
people to locate a front mounted reserve compared to a hip

00:55:58.600 --> 00:56:02.040
mounted one. But what we did see is lots of

00:56:02.040 --> 00:56:05.280
people with front mounted reserves slapping their hip

00:56:05.680 --> 00:56:07.280
because that's what they used to have.

00:56:08.840 --> 00:56:16.200
Your point about changing gear is profound and so I went in

00:56:16.200 --> 00:56:20.160
thinking front mount is best. I came out thinking actually I

00:56:20.160 --> 00:56:23.720
don't really mind, but you need to pick one thing and stick with

00:56:23.720 --> 00:56:27.440
it because when you swap between the two, that's where you're

00:56:27.440 --> 00:56:30.160
going to have a problem. And I took that on board.

00:56:30.160 --> 00:56:34.440
So I fly an air design sock which has got a front mount and

00:56:34.440 --> 00:56:38.760
I have made sure that I have a front mounted set up for my hike

00:56:38.760 --> 00:56:42.640
and fly gear as well. I think yeah, because what you

00:56:42.640 --> 00:56:44.280
said that make a choice and stick to it.

00:56:44.720 --> 00:56:48.880
Like I have been flying XR7 for the longest time and to be

00:56:48.880 --> 00:56:53.400
honest, I feel so at home in that harness because I know

00:56:53.400 --> 00:56:57.320
where and how my body, where it is touching to the harness, how

00:56:57.320 --> 00:57:00.160
it feels and everything. So I think consistency of

00:57:00.160 --> 00:57:04.440
something as important as that, like your seat, which is

00:57:04.440 --> 00:57:06.840
basically defining how you're going to make all the decisions

00:57:06.840 --> 00:57:09.240
depending on how comfortable you are, is like such an important

00:57:09.240 --> 00:57:12.040
part. So definitely I think this is

00:57:12.040 --> 00:57:15.280
what every listener should have in their mind that when they

00:57:15.280 --> 00:57:19.880
make an investment, which is fairly difficult, do I guess

00:57:19.880 --> 00:57:21.560
it's not that easy to invest that kind of money.

00:57:22.080 --> 00:57:24.800
Make sure it is something that you want and not something that

00:57:24.800 --> 00:57:28.160
you would like to sell and then move on to something else and

00:57:28.160 --> 00:57:31.280
then retune your brain wiring system until you get used to it.

00:57:33.040 --> 00:57:35.040
I think that's true. And I think if we're honest

00:57:35.040 --> 00:57:38.440
about why we make the kit decisions, we make them because

00:57:38.440 --> 00:57:43.920
of newness and kind of social environment.

00:57:43.920 --> 00:57:51.240
We make them because of cost, weight, aerodynamics, safety, I

00:57:51.240 --> 00:57:54.400
think falls down the chain a little bit.

00:57:55.240 --> 00:57:58.640
And I don't know. This is again where I slightly

00:57:58.640 --> 00:58:05.000
despair of the competition scene because I come back to this,

00:58:05.000 --> 00:58:09.520
that people in comps aren't paid and no one knows who you are

00:58:09.520 --> 00:58:11.240
except for the other people in comps.

00:58:11.720 --> 00:58:14.640
And if everybody's got a submarine, no one has an

00:58:14.640 --> 00:58:16.520
advantage for flying a submarine.

00:58:17.320 --> 00:58:22.840
So why not all agree to fly something that has decent back

00:58:22.840 --> 00:58:28.400
protection and an easy to access reserve container?

00:58:28.640 --> 00:58:31.440
But as I say, that's just me. I think maybe if I was a bit

00:58:31.440 --> 00:58:33.200
more competitively minded I might think.

00:58:33.400 --> 00:58:35.200
Like what you said makes complete sense.

00:58:35.640 --> 00:58:39.600
The whole idea of flying the top equipment is to gain the extra

00:58:39.600 --> 00:58:43.240
edge, but if that is negated by the whole field being upgraded,

00:58:43.600 --> 00:58:45.960
then what's the point of risking the upgrade?

00:58:47.800 --> 00:58:51.440
Exactly, and that's why I think SRS is such a nice way to do

00:58:51.440 --> 00:58:54.280
competitions. Brett is a difficult man to get

00:58:54.280 --> 00:59:00.800
hold of these days. He's super busy with the good.

00:59:00.800 --> 00:59:03.040
More competition, more. SRS.

00:59:03.400 --> 00:59:08.720
Yeah, Matt, there's a lot we can keep on bouncing our thoughts

00:59:08.720 --> 00:59:11.000
back and forth. And especially I would like to

00:59:11.000 --> 00:59:13.840
just put this out in public domain again, that there is so

00:59:13.840 --> 00:59:18.680
much material from Matt that is available on how to in depth

00:59:18.880 --> 00:59:21.400
things on how to make sure that you're not hypoxic or cold

00:59:21.400 --> 00:59:24.720
exposure or even the first aid kit, which I think there are

00:59:24.720 --> 00:59:27.160
quite a few videos that you've done with few creators which are

00:59:27.160 --> 00:59:31.040
explaining that in depth. So just Google up Matt Doctor,

00:59:31.040 --> 00:59:34.600
Matt Wykes paragliding and you will see endless knowledge to

00:59:34.600 --> 00:59:37.400
binge on. But if we quickly have to touch

00:59:37.400 --> 00:59:41.000
upon the topic of first aid, I'm going to take the worst case

00:59:41.000 --> 00:59:44.800
layman scenario because I think somehow, including me, a lot of

00:59:44.800 --> 00:59:46.920
pilots are susceptible to falling in this where they're

00:59:46.920 --> 00:59:50.360
like, it won't happen to me. So let me just go out and fly

00:59:50.360 --> 00:59:53.200
with whatever I have and let me focus on that rather than do the

00:59:53.200 --> 00:59:56.240
homework and whatever it takes. As you said, safety takes a

00:59:56.240 --> 00:59:59.080
backseat for some reason. And that is another very

00:59:59.080 --> 01:00:00.120
interesting psychological effect.

01:00:00.320 --> 01:00:02.880
But let's say you're a pilot who has gone out.

01:00:03.640 --> 01:00:07.280
All by himself, very little contact with the outside world

01:00:07.280 --> 01:00:09.880
except the radio and the emergency whatever you can

01:00:10.280 --> 01:00:15.080
scurry off last minute. What does your first aid look

01:00:15.080 --> 01:00:18.440
like when you don't have a first aid kit in your back?

01:00:19.000 --> 01:00:22.200
I think it's your in reach, it's what your first aid looks like.

01:00:23.680 --> 01:00:26.600
One of the like interesting things about Paradigm First Aid,

01:00:26.600 --> 01:00:29.880
which is why I find it fascinating and what I've delved

01:00:29.880 --> 01:00:34.720
into it is most first aid stuff is either kind of first aid at

01:00:34.720 --> 01:00:39.440
work, which is designed for cities and it's designed for.

01:00:40.320 --> 01:00:43.680
I've splashed this in my eye or I've cut myself or I've fallen

01:00:43.680 --> 01:00:48.560
off a ladder or it comes out of military stuff and military

01:00:48.560 --> 01:00:53.520
stuff is really first aid is one has some very specific injuries,

01:00:53.520 --> 01:00:56.880
ballistic injuries, but also it's very much part of an

01:00:56.880 --> 01:01:00.760
organised chain. Military first aid assumes that

01:01:01.000 --> 01:01:03.280
a bunch of other things are going to happen in rapid

01:01:03.280 --> 01:01:04.880
succession. Someone's going to get picked up

01:01:04.880 --> 01:01:08.440
and shipped out. So paragliding first aid then

01:01:08.440 --> 01:01:10.520
becomes this kind of really interesting hybrid.

01:01:10.520 --> 01:01:12.600
It's a bit like what we said at the beginning about how you have

01:01:12.600 --> 01:01:14.720
to cherry pick your evidence from other sources.

01:01:15.720 --> 01:01:19.680
And sometimes, as you say, we fly alone, but more often we fly

01:01:19.680 --> 01:01:24.120
in small groups with friends and we fly in remote places.

01:01:24.120 --> 01:01:29.280
So if an accident happens, then the people who are first on

01:01:29.280 --> 01:01:33.040
scene are either you, the person who has had the accident, or

01:01:33.040 --> 01:01:35.680
your friends. And they often have to be there

01:01:35.680 --> 01:01:38.680
for quite a long time, and they need to look after things that

01:01:38.680 --> 01:01:42.800
are quite serious. And the environments themselves

01:01:43.480 --> 01:01:46.000
and the rescue services aren't necessarily predictable.

01:01:46.280 --> 01:01:48.760
So it's not like first aid at work in a city, and it's not

01:01:48.760 --> 01:01:53.200
like military injuries. So then the question is, what

01:01:53.200 --> 01:01:58.080
general principles can we apply under those circumstances that

01:01:58.080 --> 01:02:01.680
will be helpful and won't make things anything worse?

01:02:02.800 --> 01:02:08.560
And so a lot of it for me is really about organization

01:02:09.640 --> 01:02:12.360
because to your point about if you're on your own, you don't

01:02:12.360 --> 01:02:15.520
have any kit, what can you do? We can get someone to come to

01:02:15.520 --> 01:02:19.880
you who does. So when I think about managing

01:02:20.400 --> 01:02:22.520
an accident or how we should think about managing an

01:02:22.520 --> 01:02:26.520
accident, the first thing that we need to do when an accident

01:02:26.520 --> 01:02:32.160
happens is establish who we have and establish A-Team.

01:02:32.640 --> 01:02:36.400
So what you sometimes see in accidents is as an accident,

01:02:36.400 --> 01:02:38.360
everyone stands back and suddenly everyone rushes to the

01:02:38.360 --> 01:02:42.240
casualty, then no one knows what to do and then suddenly a

01:02:42.240 --> 01:02:45.120
functional team will emerge. But we need to short circuit

01:02:45.120 --> 01:02:47.080
that and become a functional team straight away.

01:02:47.080 --> 01:02:50.080
So that's where talking to your friends, you say, not ignoring

01:02:50.080 --> 01:02:52.280
safety side of things, talking to your friends about what will

01:02:52.280 --> 01:02:54.800
happen if there's an accident, mentally rehearsing these

01:02:54.800 --> 01:02:57.560
things, all of that stuff that helps short circuit that process

01:02:57.560 --> 01:03:02.640
and establish A functional team. So it's functional team, it's

01:03:02.640 --> 01:03:07.840
making the scene safe, so securing the wing, not tumbling

01:03:07.840 --> 01:03:10.560
down, not landing in exactly the same place as where the other

01:03:10.560 --> 01:03:12.040
pilot landed. So the same thing that can

01:03:12.040 --> 01:03:14.680
happen to you. All of the things to make sure

01:03:14.680 --> 01:03:18.120
that you are an effective rescue team, not extra casualties.

01:03:18.840 --> 01:03:23.560
Then locate the pilot, find safe access to them, secure them so

01:03:23.560 --> 01:03:27.160
they, the pilot don't fall down a Cliff or fall out of a tree

01:03:28.160 --> 01:03:30.480
and get help. And there's been a million

01:03:30.480 --> 01:03:33.040
podcasts and things about how to get help, and it depends where

01:03:33.040 --> 01:03:35.280
you are, but just making sure you have multiple means of

01:03:35.280 --> 01:03:38.960
communication, phones, radios in reach, whatever is appropriate

01:03:38.960 --> 01:03:42.640
to where you are. So everything's secured, you're

01:03:42.640 --> 01:03:47.000
safe, the pilot is physically safe, and help is on the way.

01:03:48.040 --> 01:03:54.160
Then the next thing is that if they're bleeding, obviously work

01:03:54.160 --> 01:03:57.040
to stop it. That's most effectively done

01:03:57.040 --> 01:03:59.360
through applying direct pressure to the bleeding.

01:04:00.080 --> 01:04:02.280
In certain circumstances, tourniquets are the right thing

01:04:02.280 --> 01:04:04.560
to do. Again, huge discussions, but

01:04:04.560 --> 01:04:05.800
these are just the big principles.

01:04:06.200 --> 01:04:11.840
Stop the bleeding and then do all the usual first aid stuff,

01:04:12.080 --> 01:04:14.600
assessing them for their airway, their breathing, their

01:04:14.600 --> 01:04:18.240
circulation, all of that stuff. But the thing that's different

01:04:18.240 --> 01:04:21.280
here is protect them from the environment.

01:04:21.280 --> 01:04:25.120
We've talked a lot about cold and hypoxia and all those

01:04:25.120 --> 01:04:29.360
things. Keeping somebody warm, dry, out

01:04:29.360 --> 01:04:32.600
of the wind reduces their chance of death.

01:04:33.440 --> 01:04:36.880
It makes their blood clot more effectively if they're not cold.

01:04:37.120 --> 01:04:40.240
It reduces their energy requirements, it makes them less

01:04:40.520 --> 01:04:45.200
fearful and adrenaline ridden. So really work to make somebody

01:04:45.200 --> 01:04:48.320
comfortable. But that might mean the harness

01:04:48.320 --> 01:04:50.600
is quite good because insulation from the ground, but it might

01:04:50.600 --> 01:04:54.560
mean wrapping them in the wing. It might mean sometimes dragging

01:04:54.560 --> 01:04:56.120
them behind a rock if you have to.

01:04:56.600 --> 01:04:59.280
It might mean drying them off. Like all of these things to keep

01:04:59.280 --> 01:05:03.440
them comfortable, reassure them if you're going to move them

01:05:03.440 --> 01:05:08.720
gently and move them once planned, think ahead and then

01:05:08.760 --> 01:05:11.200
look after them as you would want to be looked after

01:05:11.200 --> 01:05:13.200
yourself. That's I think sometimes the

01:05:13.200 --> 01:05:14.840
best guidance. When people say like, how do I

01:05:14.840 --> 01:05:17.000
develop judgement? We develop judgement through

01:05:17.000 --> 01:05:18.080
experience. If you don't have the

01:05:18.080 --> 01:05:21.600
experience, then all you can do is draw on what you do have and

01:05:21.600 --> 01:05:23.680
think, if I was in this position, what would I want done

01:05:23.680 --> 01:05:25.000
for me? Go ahead.

